{"id":4813,"date":"2022-01-05T14:16:18","date_gmt":"2022-01-05T22:16:18","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=4813"},"modified":"2024-11-11T13:46:24","modified_gmt":"2024-11-11T21:46:24","slug":"3-7-color-classifier-cde","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-7-color-classifier-cde\/","title":{"rendered":"3.7: Color Sensor"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213be47d84\" name=\"6a2213be47d84\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Before You Fly<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"106\" height=\"107\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>PRE-FLIGHT WARNING<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong the longevity of your drone, avoid flying into walls or other objects. Prior to takeoff, please review lesson <em>0.2 \u201cBefore You Fly\u201d.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213be47f08\" name=\"6a2213be47f08\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1724898302765{padding-top: 20px !important;padding-right: 10px !important;padding-bottom: 20px !important;padding-left: 10px !important;background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignnone size-medium wp-image-11930\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2022\/06\/product_brand-300x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\" height=\"85\" srcset=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2022\/06\/product_brand-300x85.png 300w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2022\/06\/product_brand.png 729w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px\" \/> This lesson is currently not available in \u201cPython for Robolink\u201d.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Know the difference between HSV and RGB<\/li>\n<li>Describe how the CoDrone EDU classifies colors<\/li>\n<li>Calibrate my color sensor<\/li>\n<li>Predict colors with the CoDrone EDU<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Color Sensor Calibration<\/li>\n<li>Color Sensor Prediction<\/li>\n<li>Color Piano<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 1: Multiple Samples<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 2: Color Piano &#8211; Extended<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<a id=\"6a2213be47f79\" name=\"6a2213be47f79\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>CoDrone EDU Color Sensors<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<em>You can see colors and differentiate between them, but did you know computers can do this too?<\/em> In this lesson, you will learn how to use the CoDrone EDU&#8217;s <strong>color sensor<\/strong> to distinguish between different colors! The CoDrone EDU is equipped with two color sensors. When your CoDrone EDU is powered on, you may have noticed the two bright lights projected underneath the drone. Those lights are to help your color sensors see colors by illuminating the surface they are looking at.<\/p>\n<p>In order to get our color sensors to be the most accurate, we will first need to calibrate our color sensors. We need to calibrate them because different rooms have different brightness levels and shadows. If we want our sensors to be the most accurate, then we should calibrate them in the room we plan on using the color sensor in![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2100&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213be47fe0\" name=\"6a2213be47fe0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Using HSV Instead of RGB<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Your program will convert each RGB image to the\u00a0<strong>HSV <\/strong>color space. HSV stands for hue, saturation, and value.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hue\u00a0<\/strong>normally ranges from 0-360 and represents the color (in this application, however, it ranges from 0-180)<\/li>\n<li><strong>Saturation\u00a0<\/strong>is the color\u2019s intensity<\/li>\n<li><strong>Value\u00a0<\/strong>is how light or dark the color is<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>In computer vision applications, it is better to use the HSV color space, since it separates values for colors and intensity. This is important because shadows, reflections, and other factors may cause certain colors to look very different. The HSV color space takes this into account for more accurate results.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2102&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213be4802c\" name=\"6a2213be4802c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Remote Control Color Data<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Let&#8217;s start by finding how to access color data from the remote controller! First, make sure that you are not in the \u201cLINK\u201d state. To access color data from the controller, we need to be in remote control flight mode. Press the power button to switch between the \u201cLINK\u201d state and the \u201cRemote Control\u201d state. Once in the \u201cRemote Control\u201d state, press either the \u201cP\u201d or \u201cS\u201d button until you see the screen in the image below.\u00a0 From this screen you can see HSV values (Hue, Saturation, Value) as well as the current color seen by the drone![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;6916&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213be480fb\" name=\"6a2213be480fb\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Calibrating the Color Sensor<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In order to calibrate our color sensor, we will need to write the code ourselves! Create a new file called calibrate_color to get started.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]For this example, we will use a CoDrone EDU function to create a new directory that will hold all the color data we are about to receive. We will make a variable called <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">dataset<\/code> and assign it to the string \u201ccolor_data\u201d. Eventually we will pass this variable into a CoDrone EDU function. We will also need to create a list holding all of our landing pad colors.<\/p>\n<p><strong>NOTE:<\/strong> When calibrating the color sensor, there must be <em>at least<\/em> 3 colors in your data set. If there are less than 3 colors in the data set, an error will occur. For reference, 8 colors are included into the data set below.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">dataset = \"color_data\"\n\ncolors = [\"green\", \"purple\", \"red\", \"lightblue\", \"blue\", \"yellow\", \"black\", \"white\"]\n<\/pre>\n<p>We will now make a for loop for each color in our list. Each color will need to be calibrated, so using a loop to get through all of our colors is the most efficient way to code this! Inside our for loop, we will also need to make an empty list for our color data called <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">data<\/code>. We will also make a variable called <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">samples<\/code> that will hold the number of samples we will take for each color. We will assign samples to 500.<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">for color in colors:\n\n    data = []\n\n    samples = 500\n<\/pre>\n<p>Now we will use the input function to have our program pause. Our program will wait for the user to press enter before continuing. We will need to use this pause to set our drone on top of our desired landing pad.<\/p>\n<p>We will also be adding a loading bar to this program. This will help you see that the samples are being read and something is happening with the drone. Make sure not to move your drone while the loading bar is in progress!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">    for i in range(1):\n        print(\"Sample: \", i+1)\n        next = input(\"Press enter to calibrate \" + color)\n        print(\"0% \", end=\"\")\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Earlier, we said we wanted to take 500 samples for each color. We will use a nested for loop to take 500 samples for each color. As these samples are taken, we want to append them to our empty data list. We will also have our progress bar slowly increment as we get through this for loop by using the modulo function! Once this loop finishes, we print 100% to indicate that it is finished taking samples.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">        for j in range(samples):\n\n            color_data = drone.get_color_data()[0:9]\n\n            data.append(color_data)\n\n            time.sleep(0.005)\n\n            if j % 10 == 0:\n\n                print(\"-\", end=\"\")\n\n        print(\" 100%\")\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Once all of our color data has been gathered, we need to send it to a CoDrone EDU function that will create a new color directory for us. This will give us more accurate color data to use once we start writing color detection programs! The <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">new_color_data()<\/code> function takes a label, data, and dataset. We want to add our <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">data<\/code> list under the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">color<\/code> label in the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">dataset<\/code> folder.<\/p>\n<p>If you want to add to an existing directory instead of creating a new one you can use the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">append_color_data()<\/code> function. This function takes the same parameters as <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">new_color_data()<\/code>.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">    drone.new_color_data(color, data, dataset)\n\nprint(\"Done calibrating.\")\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Now we have a finished color classifier program! After running this program, a new directory called <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">color_data<\/code>will be created. Inside this directory will be all the text files of data for each color with hundreds of lines of data. Feel free to look at your data but make sure not to change anything! It could throw off your color detection in later programs. Here is the full code:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\nimport time\ndrone = Drone()\ndrone.pair()\n\ndataset = \"color_data\"\ncolors = [\"green\", \"purple\", \"red\", \"lightblue\", \"blue\", \"yellow\", \"black\", \"white\"]\n\nfor color in colors:\n    data = []\n    samples = 500\n\n    for i in range(1):\n        print(\"Sample: \", i+1)\n        next = input(\"Press enter to calibrate \" + color)\n        print(\"0% \", end=\"\")\n\n        for j in range(samples):\n            color_data = drone.get_color_data()[0:9]\n            data.append(color_data)\n            time.sleep(0.005)\n            if j % 10 == 0:\n                print(\"-\", end=\"\")\n        print(\" 100%\")\n\n    drone.new_color_data(color, data, dataset)\n\nprint(\"Done calibrating.\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213be48178\" name=\"6a2213be48178\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Color Prediction<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In our last example, we learned how to calibrate our color sensor for accurate color detection. In this example, we will begin predicting colors using our calibrated color sensor data!<\/p>\n<p>Now that that&#8217;s out of the way, we will move on to loading our color classifier that we just created. To do this, we will use the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.load_color_data()<\/code> function. When this function is called without a parameter, it will load the default color data in the drone. The default data isn&#8217;t always the most accurate, so instead we will use our directory that we created with our color calibration program as a parameter!<\/p>\n<p>If you followed the last example, then your directory should be called <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">color_data<\/code>. If your directory is named something else, make sure you use the name you used as a parameter to the load classifier function.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.load_color_data(\"color_data\")<\/pre>\n<p>Now that our classifier is loaded, all we have left to do is predict the color! This will be a two-step process. First, we need to get the current color data being read into our sensors. Once we get that data, we compare it to our calibrated <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">color_data<\/code> to predict the color! Since this is our first color prediction program, we will use a for loop that will print out the color your drone currently sees.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">for i in range(500):\n\n    color_data = drone.get_color_data()\n\n    color = drone.predict_colors(color_data)\n\n    print(color)\n\n    time.sleep(0.5)\n\ndrone.close()\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Your colored landing pads should be on a flat surface. Make sure that you place the CoDrone EDU directly onto the landing pads. Holding the drone above them will result in inaccurate color readings. This program will run for a few minutes and constantly be printing colors into your console. Check all the colors to see the color predictor work its magic![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213be4820e\" name=\"6a2213be4820e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Color Piano!<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Let&#8217;s make a piano out of our color predictor! In this example, we will use 4 colors to make a simple color piano. Once our piano is finished, we will be able to make music with our drone!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">From our last example we also learned that we need to load our calibrated color classifier. We will also need to do that here!<\/pre>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.load_color_data(\"color_data\")<\/pre>\n<p>Next, we will create an infinite while loop so that our drone can continuously look for colors. Inside of this while loop, we will also need to get our current color data and have the CoDrone EDU predict what color it is seeing like in our previous color prediction example.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">while True:\n    color_data = drone.get_color_data()\n    color = drone.predict_colors(color_data)<\/pre>\n<p>The <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">color<\/code> variable that we created is actually a list! <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">color[0]<\/code> holds our front color sensor&#8217;s prediction while <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">color[1]<\/code> holds the back sensor&#8217;s prediction. For this example, we will use <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">color[0]<\/code> to check for our predicted color. If our color is green, then we will play a B4 note for 1 second.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">if color[0] == \"green\":\n    drone.drone_buzzer(Note.B4, 1000)<\/pre>\n<p>Our main logic is finished, all we need to do now is add more sounds and colors. We will add an else statement that uses the buzzer but doesn&#8217;t make any sounds. Just in case!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">elif color[0] == \"red\":\n    drone.drone_buzzer(Note.A4, 1000)\nelif color[0] == \"yellow\":\n    drone.drone_buzzer(Note.C4, 1000)\nelif color[0] == \"black\":\n    drone.drone_buzzer(Note.G4, 1000)\nelse:\n    drone.drone_buzzer(0, 0)<\/pre>\n<p>Lastly, we will add a print so that we can see what color is being predicted, as well as a sleep so we don&#8217;t send too many signals to the color sensor at once.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">print(color[0])\ntime.sleep(0.2)<\/pre>\n<p>That&#8217;s all for the color piano! Here is the completed code:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\nimport time\ndrone = Drone()\ndrone.pair()\n\ndrone.load_color_data(\"color_data\")\n\nwhile True:\n    color_data = drone.get_color_data()\n    color = drone.predict_colors(color_data)\n\n    if color[0] == \"green\":\n        drone.drone_buzzer(Note.B4, 1000)\n    elif color[0] == \"red\":\n        drone.drone_buzzer(Note.A4, 1000)\n    elif color[0] == \"yellow\":\n        drone.drone_buzzer(Note.C4, 1000)\n    elif color[0] == \"black\":\n        drone.drone_buzzer(Note.G4, 1000)\n    else:\n        drone.drone_buzzer(0, 0)\n\n    print(color[0])\n    time.sleep(0.2)<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213be48267\" name=\"6a2213be48267\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenges<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Challenge 1: In our calibrate_color program we took 500 samples one time for each color. <em>Can you figure out how to change the code to take multiple samples of 500 for each color?<\/em><\/p>\n<p><strong>Hint:<\/strong> <em>Only one thing needs to be changed!<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Challenge 2: We created a basic color piano with 4 keys. Add onto it and make a color piano that includes all of your colored landing pads! You can also choose notes that are in your favorite song and try to recreate it![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213be482b0\" name=\"6a2213be482b0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learned how to use your CoDrone EDU&#8217;s color sensor to detect different colors. Using this data, you learned how to calibrate your drone to predict what color is being detected![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7546&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 11 Before You Fly [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] PRE-FLIGHT WARNING Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-7-color-classifier-cde\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4735,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3875,"_lesson_preview":""},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[4328,14401,2134,2189],"class_list":["post-4813","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-sensors","module-advanced","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4813","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":58,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4813\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":13222,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4813\/revisions\/13222"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4735"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=4813"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=4813"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=4813"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}